findings.finding:
My current finding is that a shifty function α exists iff its generating polynomial  

\[
A(x)=\sum_{m=0}^{8}\alpha(m)x^{m}
\]

can be written as a monomial \(x^{a}\) (with \(0\le a\le8\)) times a divisor \(A_{0}(x)\) of a binomial \(1+x^{d}\) (for some integer \(d>0\)) in \(\mathbb Z[x]\); equivalently  

\[
A(x)C(x)=x^{k}+x^{l}
\]

for some integer Laurent polynomial \(C(x)\).  Thus the problem reduces to counting all monic integer‑coefficient divisors \(A_{0}\) of some \(1+x^{d}\) whose degree ≤ 8, and for each such \(A_{0}\) there are \(9-\deg A_{0}\) possible shifts \(a\).  (The constant divisor 1 is allowed, giving 9 shifts.)
